messages.content:
You are participating in the first, blinded stage of a reproducible experiment
about the textual basis for interpreting the Hebrew Bible.

No disputed biblical symbol or proposed interpretation is before you. Commit
to a general rule now, before any downstream argument is shown.

Using manuscript history, the structure of the Masoretic text, the biblical
textual witnesses, and standard Hebrew/Aramaic lexicons—but not denominational
doctrine, commentaries, or the popularity of an interpretation—distinguish:

1. the consonantal written text;
2. vowel letters contained within that text;
3. Masoretic vowel points;
4. Masoretic cantillation and accent marks;
5. Ketiv and Qere readings;
6. alternate ancient readings witnessed by manuscripts or translations.

Determine:

- whether the Masoretic vowel points were part of the original written text;
- whether the points are binding on interpretation and, if not, what weight
  they carry;
- whether a grammatically and lexically valid alternate vocalization of the
  same consonants may be considered;
- what evidence must support an alternate vocalization;
- whether the received pointing alone can disqualify an alternate reading;
- how Ketiv/Qere, parallel passages, ancient translations, and manuscript
  evidence affect the decision;
- who bears the burden of proof when departing from the received pointing.

Distinguish historical findings from theological assumptions. Do not analyze,
name, or allude to any particular disputed symbol. Your structured response
will be frozen and returned to you in a later stage.
